:1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"C041\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"The answer opens with 'No.' It says exchange volume 'is not a ceiling on what can trade' and explains that APs create shares by delivering a basket and redeem them for the basket. This ties primary-market capacity to why secondary volume alone is insufficient.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"It lists 'bid–ask spread, quote depth and size', 'underlying-security liquidity and trading conditions' and 'market makers' ability to hedge'. It also cites 'creation/redemption basket details and recent flows', which reflect AP and market-maker activity. All three elements of the bundle are covered.\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":null,\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"Both required minimums are fully covered. The answer explicitly rejects volume as a cap and says the indicators are 'not assurance of execution capacity', so it avoids both central-error forms. The other statements (premiums/discounts to NAV or iNAV, checking conditions at order time) are accurate, so there is no material local falsehood.\"}","ttft_ms":2986,"type":"result","duration_ms":5919,"uuid":"c58fe45b-0f7a-4f67-9707-779cfcbdee23","ttft_stream_ms":1058,"time_to_request_ms":54,"first_content_frame_ms":1058,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
